  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nDry and pleasant days with chilly nights will continue through \nSaturday. High temperatures each afternoon will be in the 50s\nand 60s with lows dropping into the 30s and 40s each morning. \n\nGuidance has not been accurate with afternoon dew points, so did\nlower them closer to the 10-25th percentiles. RH percentages in\nthe 20s and lower 30s will be observed across much of the area\nthis afternoon and Saturday afternoon with mountain valleys\npossibly dropping into the teens. Fire weather will be limited\nthough despite these very low RH values as winds will be light\ntoday and Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA low tracking up the coast of the eastern CONUS will deliver\nrain across our mountain counties. The track is starting to move\na bit further inland leading to more confidence in chances\n(30-50%) for rainfall across our eastern periphery counties.\nRainfall amounts generally look low at this time, but portions \nof Pocahontas and Randolph counties could see rainfall amounts \nup to a quarter of an inch. This is based on the current path of\nthe coastal low though and any deviation with future runs could\ndefinitely impact this outcome. Confidence at this time is\nmedium. \n\nA cold front then moves through late Sunday through Monday,\nwhich could bring a round of light rain across the entire \nforecast area. Confidence is higher for this system as models\nall show the front crossing with a round of light rainfall\nmoving west to east Monday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nAfter the Monday cold front, colder temperatures will arrive and\nremain in place through the holiday weekend. Dry weather is\nexpected Tuesday, but uncertainty remains after that. Models all\nshow a vigorous low pressure system that will move through late\nWednesday and Thanksgiving Day. However, models diverge in\ntiming and placement of the low which will impact quite a few\ndetails. \n\nNonetheless, active weather is a possibility on Thanksgiving \nDay with at least some rain for most locations and maybe some \nsnow across the mountains. This is still far down the line and \nmany details need to be ironed out amongst the models before\nconfidence increases.",
